## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to handle mixed text and JSON logs, manage log rotation and compression, and perform cross-host aggregation with time-based filtering. It tests regex parsing, time-windowing, deduplication logic, and working within POSIX shell constraints without external dependencies.

## Key Concepts
- POSIX shell constraints with gzipped logs and mixed formats
- Time-window filtering for the last 30 minutes
- Robust user extraction from both plaintext and JSON logs
- Case-insensitive deduplication with per-host aggregation

## Code Example
```bash
for host in $(awk -F'[/=]' '/^\/(logs|var)\/log\// && !/^#/ {print $3}' /etc/hosts 2>/dev/null | sort -u); do
  users=$(find "/logs" -name '*.log*' -mmin -30 2>/dev/null | \
    xargs -I{} sh -c 'zcat -f "{}" 2>/dev/null' | \
    awk -v host="$host" '
      BEGIN {now=systime(); cutoff=now-1800}
      /^[[:space:]]/ {next}
      /^"?[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
        gsub(/[\[\]:]/, "", $1" "$2)
        cmd="date -d \""$1" "$2"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
        cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
        if (ts >= cutoff) {
          if ($0 ~ /ERROR|WARN/) {
            for (i=1; i<=NF; i++) {
              if ($i ~ /^user[=:]/) {
                gsub(/[,:"\047]/, "", $(i+1))
                print tolower($(i+1))
              }
            }
          }
        }
        next
      }
      /^"?\{/ {
        if (match($0, /"ts"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, t)) {
          cmd="date -d \""t[1]"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
          cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
          if (ts >= cutoff && match($0, /"user"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, u)) {
            print tolower(u[1])
          }
        }
        next
      }
    ' | sort -u | wc -l)
  echo "$host: $users unique users"
done
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical log parsing under rotation, mixed formats, gz archives, and multi-line blocks. It also checks ability to design robust pipelines with standard UNIX tools.

## Key Concepts
- POSIX text processing (awk, sed)
- gzipped logs via zcat
- JSON vs text parsing
- multi-line block handling
- time bucketing by hour

## Code Example
```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
cutoff=$(date -d '6 hours ago' '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
zcat /logs/app/**/*.log.gz /logs/app/**/*.log 2>/dev/null | awk -v c="$cutoff" '
  function hour(ts){ return substr(ts,1,13) }
  {
    if($0 ~ /"ts":/) { match($0, /"ts":"([^\"]+)"/, m); ts=m[1]; match($0, /"src":"([^\"]+)"/, s); ip=s[1] }
    else if($0 ~ /^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/) { ts=$1" "$2 }
    if(ts && ip && /FAILED/){ h=hour(ts); buckets[h][ip]=1 }
  }
  END{ for (h in buckets) print h, length(buckets[h]) }
'
```

